WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.330
- Welcome to the Lifespan Podcast,

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.610
where we discuss the science of aging,

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.893
and how to be healthier
at any stage of life.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.320
I'm David Sinclair.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.480
I'm a professor at Harvard Medical School.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.180
I run a research lab that studies aging,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.100
why we get old and why we may not have to.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.880
I've been there for 20 years,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.900
and we've made some breakthroughs

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.520
we're going to talk about on this podcast.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.640
I'm also joined by my lovely
co-host, Matt LaPlante

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.840
- Lovely, talented, brilliant-

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.680
- Reasonably talented...
- Good looking.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.780
- You're a great writer.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.890
We co-wrote a book
called Lifespan together.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.790
- I'll take that compliment.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.640
Thank you.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.160
- And we've been really excited

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.010
about the reception of
this book, but we thought,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.900
why don't we spread the word.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.780
Those of you who haven't
read the book, that's fine.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.160
We're going to go into
detail in this episode,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.410
to give you a basis

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.380
for what we're going to
talk about in this series.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.320
- And if you have read
the book, that's okay too,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.180
because so much has changed
in the last three years

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.610
since the book was published.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:04.677
- Right, so a little bit about you,

00:01:04.677 --> 00:01:07.370
before we get too much into the weeds.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.610
You're a writer.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.920
You used to be reporter.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.300
You've been a war correspondent.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.880
What am I missing?

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.750
- I'm a professor of journalism
at Utah State University.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.820
- Right, it's not Harvard
University, but it's -

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.513
- It's the Harvard University
of the Mountain West.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.380
- Anyway, you're brilliant at writing,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.600
and I certainly couldn't have
written this book without you.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.790
But this podcast...

00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:29.550
We talk a lot every week.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.610
- Yeah.
- We're writing another book,

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.060
which will be due out, we
hope, probably next year?

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.800
- Oh dear God.[laughs]
- Yeah, well..

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.690
We're going as fast as we can.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.800
So Matt and I talk a lot,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.130
and we have these conversations
that are pretty funny.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.560
And anyone who overhears
these conversations

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.890
says "That should be a podcast."

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.020
- Wait, they're funny to us.
- Well, we'll see.[laughs]

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.210
They might be funny to other people.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.180
But the point is you
bring out the best in me.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.270
You act like the caveman

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.620
with the questions that
I need to ask myself.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.690
And that's what gave rise to Lifespan.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.120
It was asking the questions
that I didn't even know

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.120
I needed to answer.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.320
- And for me, that experience is like,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.050
and whenever I work
with anybody on a book,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.950
but especially, for whatever reason,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.020
the way we've hit it off, I feel,

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.520
coming away from these conversations,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.580
that I've just gone through
a graduate course of study.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.640
And to think that I don't
have any personal expertise,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.940
and I didn't go to
school to do what you do,

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.930
but I come away from these conversations,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.250
and I feel like I've been in class.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.950
In a really good way,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.100
cause I'm a lifelong learner.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.070
I love this, right?

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.560
I'm a student right now.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.560
And that's something that
we can share with everyone.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.840
- Well, it sounds corny,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.079
but it really is a match made in heaven.

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:46.930
Before I met you, you had
written a book about epigenetics.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:47.763
We're going to get into that.

00:02:47.763 --> 00:02:49.920
That's key to understanding aging.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.040
You'd written a book
about extreme animals.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.377
Your book, do you want to plug it, or?

00:02:55.377 --> 00:02:57.610
- "Superlative: The Biology of Extremes"?

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.443
- Yes.

00:02:58.443 --> 00:03:01.650
- Didn't sell quite as
copies as Lifespan.[laughs]

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.610
- Well, so yeah, we should plug that.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:04.550
It's a great book.
- Thank you.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.900
It's about animals that teach us

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.730
how we can optimize our biology,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.940
something that's going to
be a topic of this series.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:11.773
- Yeah, and in fact,

00:03:11.773 --> 00:03:14.460
we're going to talk about that
a little bit today as well.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.293
- Well, we are.

00:03:15.293 --> 00:03:16.250
The point of this series...

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.820
Let's talk about why are we doing this,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.010
besides entertaining ourselves and others,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.410
is that we are at a turning
point in medical history.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.370
We finally, as a species,

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.440
understand how to control our biology,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.440
how to optimize our bodies,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.110
during early life, mid-life
and certainly late life,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:37.000
to lead lives that can be
decades longer and healthier.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.900
And we want to share that information.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.920
It's very hard for the
average person to understand,

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.110
let alone digest, thousands
of scientific papers.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:45.943
That's what I do.

00:03:45.943 --> 00:03:48.000
I have a team of researchers
and in fact, today,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.510
we're supported by a team of researchers

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.420
who have helped us gather information

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.000
from around the scientific literature.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
My job, and yours, is to present
that as a lesson to people

00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:01.260
who want to listen and learn,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.890
beyond what they hear and
can read on the internet,

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.593
which is actually rather
untrustworthy information.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.450
I promise to be a source
of facts and information.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.390
When we give a study,
people can look it up.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.740
If I don't know something,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.530
we will make sure that we
find the original source

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:20.960
to that information,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.150
so that you can trust if
we say something as a fact,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.180
it is definitely a fact.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.730
And we're not selling anything.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.330
I'm not selling any supplement.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.650
So when I talk about supplements
later, it's based on facts.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.670
And we promise to say whether
it's a study in a worm,

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.540
or a yeast cell or a mouse or a human,

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:41.380
'cause that's very important,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.620
'cause otherwise people
conflate a study in a mouse

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:45.453
with a human study.

00:04:45.453 --> 00:04:47.680
And there's a big difference
between a mouse and a human,

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.513
of course.

00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:51.290
- All right, so we're going
to do this for eight episodes.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.046
Today's episode is Why We Age.

00:04:54.046 --> 00:04:55.790
We're going to dive into
that in a second here,

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.450
but let's give a real brief overview

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.170
of where we're going from here.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.760
- Well, the reason for
doing this podcast really

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.530
is that we are living in
a really important moment

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.850
