00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.400
That's levels.link/sinclair.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:49.900
- David, the research in this space

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.810
is moving really fast right now.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.100
It's hard to keep up with.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.300
It's hard even for you to
keep up with sometimes, right?

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:58.133
- Well, it is.

00:10:58.133 --> 00:11:01.140
I read scientific papers
even before I get out of bed.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.660
I'm here to collate that and present it.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.180
But there's so many publications
that come out every week

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.730
that it's making my head spin.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.730
And the technology
that's being developed...

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.040
We can do a million experiments

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.160
that each one used to take a year,

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:16.310
when I started in this field.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:20.610
- And so that's just allowing
publications to flourish

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.320
right now in this area?

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.760
It's allowing researchers
to do a lot more work

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.340
in a shorter amount of time.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:31.700
The result is that there's
a lot to go through.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.150
- Well, there is, and it's
coming out every week.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:38.710
And so the goal here is to
keep everybody up to speed

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:42.210
on what the latest
discoveries, technology,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:43.470
and what they can apply to their lives,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.800
as we're learning this
almost on a daily basis.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.010
- And this is the Lifespan Podcast,

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:52.950
but if you haven't read
Lifespan, it's okay, right?

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.864
We're going to get you to speed-
- It's not okay.

00:11:54.864 --> 00:11:55.720
[David and Matthew laughing]
- You should.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:56.920
If you haven't read Lifespan,

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.403
you should read Lifespan.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:02.090
You don't have to pause the
video right now, though.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:02.923
- Right.

00:12:02.923 --> 00:12:04.340
We're going to give everybody a primer

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:05.970
on what's in Lifespan.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:07.440
And if you've read the book, great.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:08.880
We're going to go over the major topics,

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.580
so that everybody is with us,

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.630
as we go forward with
these other seven episodes.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:14.720
- And even if you've read the book,

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:15.830
there's more information.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.030
I mean, there's been a couple of years

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.430
since the book was published.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:19.800
- Oh yeah, we're light years ahead

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.113
of where we were in Lifespan.

00:12:21.113 --> 00:12:23.730
There's a lot to talk about,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.950
including things that you can
apply in your daily lives,

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.830
that we didn't know when
Lifespan was written.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.160
- One of misconceptions I
think a lot of people have

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:32.020
about the book, a lot of people
have about your research,

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:36.010
is they think that you're
trying to prevent old age?

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.350
- Well, we are, but
that's not the only thing.

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:39.810
- Because what we're
talking about, really,

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.230
is preventing aging,

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:45.060
and that doesn't just
happen when we are old.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:45.893
- Of course not.

00:12:45.893 --> 00:12:48.690
In fact, it happens over
your whole entire lifespan,

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.520
even before you're born.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.750
- We're going to get into
exactly how we know that,

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:53.583
but this is fascinating.

00:12:53.583 --> 00:12:55.440
This is a fascinating area of research.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.940
There's been recent studies on this,

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:01.940
which can actually measure,
in utero, an infant's age.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:02.910
- Yeah, in fact, when we're young,

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.230
we're aging faster than when we're old.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:06.670
But we'll talk about that clock later.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:09.940
But what's important here is
that we can improve our health

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.150
and our long-term survival at any age.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.910
And we're going to talk
about those lessons,

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.180
and what people can do at any age

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:20.000
to improve their chances of
being healthy later in life,

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.360
as well as look good and
function optimally now.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:26.370
- Right, there's a lot of misconceptions

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.420
about what aging is.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.510
I think in no small part

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:35.510
that comes from the conflation
of aging with years lived.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:39.180
That makes sense for most
of our human history,

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:43.130
because people got old
when they were older,

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:45.273
or at least as we conceptualize it, older.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:50.500
But the other thing that
people have long assumed

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:53.080
is that aging is inevitable.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:54.720
It's just going to happen to all of us.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:59.053
You have argued to me,
and I think I believe you,

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:01.200
this is not the case?

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:02.670
- Right, and in my lab now,

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.200
we can control aging
very precisely at will.

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:07.890
We can speed it up as fast
as we want in an animal,

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.270
and even reverse it.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:10.680
So aging is now controllable.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.320
We have the technology to
control how fast we age.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.010
We can measure that, slow
it down and even reverse it.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.660
This has never happened
in human history before.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:21.610
And one of the reasons
that we're doing this today

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:25.250
is to make sure everybody
knows that this is coming,

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:26.810
because it's going to fundamentally change

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.820
the course of human history.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:31.510
- Is it something that
perhaps shouldn't have come

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:35.860
as such a shock to us?

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:40.610
Because in fact, if you look
across the animal kingdom,

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:44.970
there are all kinds of animals
that age at different speeds,

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:45.803
at different rates?

00:14:45.803 --> 00:14:47.630
Even though, as we'll get into,

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:51.410
the root source of aging
is the same for everything,

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.330
we age at different rates?

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.430
- Well, we do, and
sometimes I'm criticized

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:59.020
because I'm on record
saying the first person

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:02.440
to make it to 150 years of
age has already been born.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:04.087
And people say, "How can you say that?"

00:15:04.087 --> 00:15:05.240
"What gives you confidence

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.630
that we can even live five years longer?"

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.390
Well, if we look at the
animal kingdom, first of all,

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:12.350
there are plenty of species
that live that long.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:14.870
There is no reason why
we can't do what they do.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:16.030
And if we learn how they do it,

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.750
we can apply that to ourselves.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.860
- So one of these animals

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:23.630
that I know you're
fascinated by right now,

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:24.930
in fact, there's a picture of them

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:27.268
on your computer screen right now.

00:15:27.268 --> 00:15:28.540
One of your children, of course, on these.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:31.300
- Yeah, Alex, our oldest child, yeah.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:33.900
These naked mole-rats,
they're fascinating.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:34.733
They're weird-looking.

00:15:34.733 --> 00:15:37.030
They got sharp teeth and the
rest of the body is long.

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:39.392
It looks like a condom
filled with water, actually-

00:15:39.392 --> 00:15:40.790
[Matthew laughing]

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:41.820
Yeah, they're really weird,

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.763
but what's exciting is to work on them.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.420
And Vera Gorbunova is the professor

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.220
that Alex works with.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:53.200
They found that these animals
have particular traits,

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.110
biologically, molecularly,

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:57.070
that allow them to live decades longer

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:58.680
than a typical rodent.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.950
Mice live a bit over two years,
rats, over four years, but-

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:05.570
- These are similar
animals and one lives...

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:07.210
How long does a mole-rat live?

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.043
- Over 30 years.

00:16:08.043 --> 00:16:11.080
- Over 30 years and then normal
rodents or other rodents,

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:13.180
most rodents, live a few, right?

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.580
- Right, and what's important to realize

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.510
is that there's a reason why that is.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:20.160
Animals that don't have
a lot of predation,

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.740
whales, mollusks,

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.570
sharks, mole-rats underground,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.940
they don't get eaten,

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:27.640
so they can put their energy

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:29.840
into building a long-lasting body,

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.940
and actually, as a consequence,

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.000
they reproduce slowly as well.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:34.833
But what's important

00:16:34.833 --> 00:16:36.930
is that you can build a longer-lived body.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:37.940
And we can do that too.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:39.060
We can engineer ourselves.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.380
We can learn from these animals,

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.700
by studying what makes them live so long.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.910
- Well, you just named some

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:48.010
of the pretty well-known
longevity species.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:48.843
Can we get into that?

00:16:48.843 --> 00:16:50.010
Can we talk about

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:55.010
what makes each of these
animals survive for so long?

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:56.510
- Right, we can learn a lot.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.320
In fact, we should also talk
about the bristlecone pine,

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:01.560
which was a species you
were going to look at

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.540
when I first met you,
before we wrote the book.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:04.670
- I was, I was in the White Mountains.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:06.080
Actually, the first
time you called me ever,

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:08.497
I was up on my way to go see

00:17:08.497 --> 00:17:10.650
the bristlecone pines
in the White Mountains.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.200
- Right, and so these,
how long do they live?

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.620
- Like 5,000 years.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:16.140
It's an absolutely incredible-
- So that's-

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.110
They were old when Jesus was born.

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:20.010
- Yeah.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:20.843
Yeah.

00:17:20.843 --> 00:17:22.843
- So what we can learn from those species

00:17:22.843 --> 00:17:25.060
is that you don't have to get old.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:26.603
- Okay, but there's a big, big difference

00:17:26.603 --> 00:17:29.170
between a bristlecone
pine and a human being,

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:31.720
or a naked mole-rat, for that matter.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:32.920
- Really?

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.330
No, we're all built from the same stuff.

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:37.280
We've got DNA, proteins, RNA.

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.640
We can engineer ourselves
to be that long-lived.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.070
If we didn't have predation
for the next 10 million years,

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:45.730
we would live for centuries.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:48.650
We just don't have time to wait
for evolution to take hold.

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.180
We can engineer ourselves
the way evolution

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.210
would make us live longer

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:55.450
- One of the interesting
things about bristlecone pines,

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:56.331
and you see this if you go anywhere

00:17:56.331 --> 00:17:59.920
where these amazing like gnarly trees are.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.047
And they really are, I mean,

00:18:02.047 --> 00:18:03.840
they actually don't really
even look like they're living

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:04.673
a lot of times.

00:18:04.673 --> 00:18:08.000
And they live in these
incredibly adverse environments.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.993
And that's characteristic of
a lot of long-lived species.

00:18:13.009 --> 00:18:14.290
Is there a clue there?

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:17.320
- Well, these species that
don't have a lot of predation

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.170
live a long time, but also in
these extreme environments,

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:23.040
they're turning on their
body's defenses against aging.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.270
And we can do that to ourselves.

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:25.750
It's a concept called hormesis,

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:27.980
and again, we'll get to
that in other episodes,

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:29.540
but adversity...

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.410
As long as it doesn't hurt your body,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.200
will make you healthy and live long.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.580
- Okay then, I do take your point

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:39.270
that there's vast similarities
across all living organisms,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:41.800
but I do think, also, it's
hard to make the argument

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:45.750
that a tree is something
that we can learn from.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:48.230
But we don't need to
even make that argument

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:51.440
cause there are these
other organisms, right?

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.000
Going from sort of like
a bristlecone pine,

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.400
which is a plant,

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:57.430
to a sponge, which is an animal,

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:00.630
to a mollusk, which is an animal,

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:03.620
to a whale, which is a mammal,

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:07.320
and we're getting closer
and closer to humans,

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:08.850
and across all of these categories,

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.330
we've got organisms that
have learned how to live

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:12.760
a lot longer than we do.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:16.120
- Right, well, we're
essentially upright whales.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:16.953
That sounds crazy,
[Matthew laughing]

00:19:16.953 --> 00:19:19.900
but at the molecular level, if
you look at the biochemistry,

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:21.870
we're only separated by 40 million years,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:24.550
which is a blink of an
eye in geological time.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:27.800
We actually could live as long as whales.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:30.000
There's not that much
difference between us.

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:34.210
- We know bowhead whales can
live for hundreds of years.

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:35.640
More than 200 years.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:36.690
- Right.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:37.523
Well, yeah.

00:19:37.523 --> 00:19:38.356
And they know that how they know that how?

00:19:38.356 --> 00:19:41.340
- They know that for a number of reasons.

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:43.210
There's actually been some
really cool studies on this,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:48.210
but one of the reasons is
that they dated a harpoon

00:19:48.310 --> 00:19:52.640
that was stuck in a bowhead
whale for 200 years.

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:53.690
And the whale was still living

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.240
when they pulled it out of the whale.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.900
Well, I think they whaled it, but...

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.810
- Yeah, well, what's interesting
is we now have the genome

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:01.940
of these animals and we can see

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:03.840
that they have multiple copies

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.760
of what we call longevity genes,

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:09.890
some of which we discovered
when I was in my twenties.

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:12.720
These are genes that protect the body.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.520
Longevity genes actually
get turned on by adversity,

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:16.930
and there are ways we can tweak them,

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:20.310
but whales naturally have multiple copies

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:23.310
and higher levels of these
longevity protective mechanisms.

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:26.330
And the bristlecone pine
has just whopping amounts.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:27.163
- The problem-

00:20:27.163 --> 00:20:28.630
- And that's that connection
that you're making there.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:29.582
You're like,

00:20:29.582 --> 00:20:30.740
"Oh, look, we see this
in a bristlecone pine,

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.560
we see this in a whale,
and oh, by the way,

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:35.580
this gene also exists in humans."

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.300
- Yeah, we're not that different
from a banana, actually.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:39.640
The genomes are 40% similar-

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:41.076
- You're not that different
from banana.[laughs]

00:20:41.076 --> 00:20:42.550
- Well...

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:43.630
I'm not going to go there, Matt.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:44.970
But the important thing is

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:47.290
that when you study other organisms,

00:20:47.290 --> 00:20:50.400
whether it's a banana or a
yeast cell or a worm or a fly,

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.000
we can learn lessons about biology,

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:54.290
and especially about aging.

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:56.493
We can learn more about
aging from a yeast cell

00:20:56.493 --> 00:20:59.110
than we can about Alzheimer's or cancer.

00:20:59.110 --> 00:21:00.680
- This is why you're dismissive

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:02.620
about people's dismissiveness

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:04.557
about like only in mice, when people say,

00:21:04.557 --> 00:21:08.050
"Oh, we discovered this." and
people go, "Oh, only in mice."

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:10.140
There's a lot we can learn from mice.

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:10.990
- Well, we can.

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:13.750
And aging is one of
these universal things,

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:16.670
and what really is remarkable
that we've learned,

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:18.550
in my lab over the last 20 years,

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:21.690
is that the same genes
that control aging in yeast

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:25.520
work in worms and flies,
mice and even humans.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:26.550
That's a big deal.

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.970
We didn't know that 30 years ago.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.440
Now we know that there's
a fundamental set of genes

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:34.080
that controls how fast we live,

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.150
and then we can also exploit that,

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:40.030
to slow down our own aging
now, and as we'll get to later,

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:43.240
we can use that knowledge to
even reverse the aging process.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.860
- We're talking about these genes.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.670
Let's name some of these genes.

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:49.470
- Right, so there are various categories.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.490
There are hundreds of
longevity genes that are known,

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:54.110
but they fall into three main buckets.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.460
The first one, it's called mTOR.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:00.430
It's a little M, for mammal,
and TOR, target of rapamycin.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:04.040
So mTOR is a longevity gene

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.330
that makes a protein
that senses amino acids.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.100
So when you eat a big steak,

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:13.007
mTOR activity actually is
activated, and mTOR now says,

00:22:13.007 --> 00:22:14.380
"Okay, I've got lots of protein.

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:15.810
I'm going to make muscle.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:17.410
I'm going to burn energy."

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:18.630
And that's why, when you eat a steak,

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:20.390
you actually build up muscle,

00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:22.290
more than if you don't
eat a lot of protein.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.090
- So is that a good thing?

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.350
- No, it's actually not.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:28.660
What you want is to have mTOR levels low,

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.020
to inhibit the mTOR.

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:32.220
And when you have low mTOR activity,

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:34.060
that's when you get the longest lifespan.

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:36.690
Mice that are given a
drug called rapamycin,

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.740
which is used to suppress the
immune system in patients,

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:42.040
but you can take low doses as a human,

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.350
or give it in low doses to a mouse.

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:46.450
Those mice live dramatically longer,

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:48.090
even if you give them rapamycin

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:49.970
when they're 20 months of age,

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:50.910
which is a really old mouse,

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:53.070
that'd be like a 75-year-old human,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.380
they still live longer.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:59.380
So low activity of mTOR is a
signal that times are tough.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:00.900
You don't have enough food.

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:03.960
Hunker down, build a
stronger body, survive.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.860
And the outcome of that is
longer life for these mice,

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:08.060
but it turns out it's not just for mice.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:12.050
You give rapamycin to a yeast
cell or a fly or a worm,

00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:12.980
they also live longer.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.110
So you can tap in with these drugs,

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:19.880
into this universal longevity
program, one of which is mTOR.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:21.610
- So you have a colleague,
a long-term colleague,

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.410
who's been working on mTOR
for a really long time,

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:26.270
and I know you're really
impressed with their work?

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:27.860
- I think you're referring
to Matt Kaeberlein?

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:28.693
- Yeah.
- Yeah,

00:23:28.693 --> 00:23:29.930
so he's doing great work.

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:31.770
His latest work that's fascinating,

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:34.040
is he's treating dogs with rapamycin,

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:36.460
and showing that it protects their heart.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:38.890
And he's also going to
test their lifespan.

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:40.440
- And we're seeing in dogs,

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:44.570
what we saw in yeast and
mice, that rapamycin works.

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:47.513
This target of rapamycin, this gene,

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:50.110
influenced in a certain way,

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:52.250
works the same way across species?

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:55.210
- Right, and that's why
I think solving aging

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.570
is going to be easier than
solving cancer and heart disease,

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.940
because aging is fundamentally conserved.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:01.720
We can make rapid progress,

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:04.150
extending the lifespan of
little yeast cells, worms,

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:06.560
with chemicals, and then
put them into humans.

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:07.470
And hopefully we'll see

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.360
they'll work the same way
like rapamycin seems to do,

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.370
and other molecules that
we're going to get to

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:13.710
in later episodes.

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:15.950
But the point here is that
aging is not that complicated,

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:19.513
once you understand the
genes that control it,

00:24:19.513 --> 00:24:21.227
and then the fundamental
causes of aging as well.

00:24:21.227 --> 00:24:22.670
- You just said genes that control it.

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:23.860
So we talked about mTOR,

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:25.670
but there are other genes that control it,

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:28.402
and we're going to see similar
things in each of these genes.

00:24:28.402 --> 00:24:30.200
What do you want to talk
about next, after mTOR?

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:31.900
- Let's do bucket two.
- Okay.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.440
- Bucket two is AMPK.

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:38.010
It's called AMP-activated kinase.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.620
So what is AMPK?

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:41.760
- It sounds like the name of a punk band.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:42.593
- Oh, that'd be a great name, actually.

00:24:42.593 --> 00:24:44.872
- Ladies and gentlemen, it's AMPK.

00:24:44.872 --> 00:24:46.000
[David laughing]

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:46.990
- And in this case,

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:48.470
we don't want less of
it, we want more of it.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:50.560
We want to activate AMPK.

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:52.060
So how do you do that?

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.410
You actually eat less.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.820
You'd fast and that'll turn on AMPK.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:56.653
It'll do a few things.

00:24:56.653 --> 00:24:58.970
It'll make your body more
sensitive to insulin,

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:00.940
suck the sugar out of your bloodstream,

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:02.920
which can be toxic if it stays high.

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:04.330
But it also ramps up

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:06.720
the energy-producing centers of the body.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:08.390
These membrane-filled bags,

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:11.120
which are actually ancient
bacteria living in our bodies,

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.640
called mitochondria.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:16.707
- Who's doing the cool
work and AMP A-Q-R-S-T...

00:25:16.707 --> 00:25:18.320
[David laughing]
AMPK?

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:19.780
Who's doing the cool
work in AMPK right now?

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:21.900
- Well, it's been studied
by thousands of labs,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:24.930
but the one that's most
interesting, I think right now,

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:29.000
is to see if a drug that activates AMPK

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.450
extends human lifespan, slows down aging,

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:34.380
and that's Nir Barzilai, my
good friend down in New York,

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:36.010
at Albert Einstein College of Medicine.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:37.200
- And the drug is metformin,

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:40.880
which most people would
know as a diabetes drug?

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:42.070
- Yeah, metformin's really interesting,

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:43.690
because it's super safe.

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:45.990
It's one of the safest drugs ever known.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:48.390
It's on the World Health Organization's

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:50.750
List of Essential Medicines for humanity.

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:53.690
In much of the world it's cost pennies,

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:54.750
and you can get it over the counter-

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.400
- Are they paying you to take it?

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.520
- Essentially, in most countries,

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.470
they just want you to have like aspirin.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.030
In the US and a lot of
English-speaking countries,

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:05.420
and Europe, you need a
prescription for this drug.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.420
And it's the frontline
therapy for Type 2 diabetes,

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:10.140
or high blood sugar.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:14.940
- And there is an active,
ongoing human trial

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:18.150
of metformin called Targeting
Aging with Metformin, right?

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:19.830
The TAME study?
- The TAME study, yeah?

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:20.663
- [ Matthew] Yeah.

00:26:20.663 --> 00:26:22.630
- So why would we think that
metformin actually works?

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:24.890
Well, first is my lab,

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:26.850
in collaboration with Rafael de Cabo,

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:29.100
down at the National Institute on Aging,

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:31.460
showed in a mouse study that
if you give them metformin,

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.320
those mice are healthier and live longer.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:35.410
Again, doesn't prove
that it works in humans,

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.210
but you can take tens of thousands...

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:39.520
And people have taken tens
of thousands of people

00:26:39.520 --> 00:26:41.320
who are on metformin for Type 2 diabetes,

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:44.190
and looked at their overall
health and their lifespan,

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:47.650
and this fact that I'll
tell you blows my mind.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:51.130
Type 2 diabetics that go
on metformin, on average,

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:54.920
live longer than people that
don't have Type 2 diabetes.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:56.950
And they're protected against diseases.

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:57.830
So if you're susceptible

00:26:57.830 --> 00:26:59.710
to cancer, or heart disease, frailty,

00:26:59.710 --> 00:27:02.820
those on metformin reduce
their risk of those diseases,

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:04.610
whereas those that don't take metformin,

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:05.830
it increases over time.

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:08.740
- Okay, why does Nir think
that this is happening?

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:12.340
Like, at the molecular
level, what's going on?

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:15.130
- Well, AMPK is a sensor of energy,

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:18.007
and low energy is a good
thing for longevity.

00:27:18.007 --> 00:27:19.620
That's one of the reasons I fast.

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:22.360
We'll get into that in a future episode.

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:25.370
But AMPK is central to sensing low energy.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:28.210
And in response, it protects the body,

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.540
because it's thinking we're
going to run out of food.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.700
So this is another evidence
of this term, hormesis.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:34.990
What doesn't kill you, makes you stronger,

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.990
and AMPK sits as a central
regulator of the body's defenses.

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.350
It will down-regulate mTOR,

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:44.240
it will activate other longevity genes,

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:45.073
and that's really important-

00:27:45.073 --> 00:27:46.900
- So these genes are working
in concert with one another?

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.150
- That's a really important concept,

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.360
because about a decade ago,

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:52.030
scientists like me, we were fighting

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:53.981
over whose longevity
gene was more important.

00:27:53.981 --> 00:27:55.910
[Matthew laughing]
It was pretty pathetic.

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.440
- And very typical of scientists.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:27:59.273
- Right.

00:27:59.273 --> 00:28:01.680
Everyone wants to be the
smartest person in the room.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.740
But what we finally
realized, 10 years ago,

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:05.590
is that these genes talk to each other

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:07.560
and they're part of a network,

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:10.360
which actually is a blessing and a curse,

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:14.170
because you can tweak one
and affect the others.

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:18.400
But now we're faced with
which one is best to tweak,

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:20.900
and if you tweak two out of
three, or three out of three,

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:22.760
is that good or is it bad?

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:24.610
And that we'll get to,
also, in later episodes.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:26.570
- Well, okay, and then
if you said three now,

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:28.260
so I'm hearing some similar things,

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:30.200
and I think this is really important,

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:34.763
one of which is that these
genes are activated by stress.

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:36.663
mTOR is.

00:28:39.383 --> 00:28:41.830
AMPK is.
- Yeah.

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:44.890
- And the gene that you've been working on

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:47.640
for a really long time, is as well.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:49.130
- Well, it's a family of genes.

00:28:49.130 --> 00:28:51.430
We have seven of these,
what we call sirtuins,

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:55.250
and we linked them to aging

00:28:55.250 --> 00:28:58.690
when I was a postdoc at
MIT, back in the 1990s.

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:01.370
And what we were looking at, as a group,

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.340
was what can make yeast cells live longer?

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:05.390
And there was one mutation

00:29:05.390 --> 00:29:09.283
that led us to a gene called SIR2, S-I-R2,

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:13.990
which stands for silent
information regulator number two.

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:16.140
And information is the key,

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:17.550
but we'll get to that later.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.980
But this set of sirtuin
genes, this family,

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:24.900
has been shown in yeast
and worms and flies, mice,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:26.120
and now humans,

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:29.800
to be a really important
central regulator of longevity.

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:33.840
We showed, now going back to 2005,

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:36.270
that fasting activates the sirtuins,

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:39.340
and later, we showed exercise
activates these genes.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:42.570
So a lot of the things that
were told by doctors to do,

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:44.850
to live longer and be healthier,

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.150
we think acts through the sirtuins,

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.000
in concert with these
other two sets of genes

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:50.160
we just talked about.

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.123
- You know what I really
appreciate about this,

00:29:52.123 --> 00:29:55.730
cause I think there's a lot of
bagging on doctors right now,

00:29:55.730 --> 00:29:57.060
It's like, "They never
tell us the same thing.

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:58.000
They never tell us the same thing."

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.040
But they've always told
us diet and exercise.

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:01.960
And maybe like the individualized advice

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:04.160
is a little different, or maybe
it's shifted a little bit,

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:07.000
but it's like eat better, work out.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:10.440
And that's what helps
activate the sirtuins

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:11.660
to do what they do.

00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:13.000
- Yeah, and it's an important concept,

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.120
'cause most people think
when you run your blood flows

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:17.240
and clears out your arteries,

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:18.640
and when you eat less,

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:19.740
it's just good for you because-

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.017
- But that's not what's happening?

00:30:21.017 --> 00:30:21.850
- Well, right.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.130
What we've discovered is
that these longevity genes,

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:26.640
these three sets we've just mentioned,

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:30.530
need to be either
down-regulated or up-regulated,

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:31.540
in a certain way.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:32.510
How do you do that?

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:35.410
These lifestyles, certain types of diet,

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:37.490
when you eat and exercise.

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:38.940
Essentially, you want to trick your body

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:41.180
into thinking that it's
under threat of survival.

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:42.240
Adversity.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:43.080
You don't want it too much.

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:44.250
You don't want to damage your body,

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:46.890
so that it can actually
have the opposite effect,

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:50.210
but you want to give it
a little bit of fear.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:53.950
And it will have great results,

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:57.080
and give you payback for decades to come.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:59.220
- Okay, so I know we've talked about

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:02.390
these other two longevity genes,

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:05.160
but because you're the sirtuin guy,

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:06.750
I want to dive a little deeper

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:10.640
into what's making these things tick.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:12.660
And this gets a little complicated.

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:13.900
It's a little Down in the Weed-ish,

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:14.900
but it's important,

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:18.433
as we get to the
information theory of aging,

00:31:19.493 --> 00:31:21.180
and this idea that we're
going to be driving toward

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.690
in a minute here, which
is X differentiation.

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:26.480
So can we talk a little bit about

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:30.910
why the sirtuins are
doing what they're doing,

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:33.330
and how that works at the genetic level?

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:34.163
- Right, all right.

00:31:34.163 --> 00:31:36.350
Well, let's do a little bit of biology.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:39.080
Some people haven't had a
biology lesson in decades.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:39.920
- It's been a long time.

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:41.180
- Yeah.

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:42.970
So let's start with a cell.

00:31:42.970 --> 00:31:46.640
Cell is a microscopic bag of
fluid packed with proteins.

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:48.480
The proteins are encoded by the DNA,

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:51.140
which is in a part of the
cell called the nucleus.

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:52.530
So let's become microscopic.

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:55.200
Let's dive down into a cell
and have a look what's there.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:57.690
So, first of all, we
pierce the outer membrane.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:59.690
There are a lot of
proteins on the surface,

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.080
that bring in things like
sugar and amino acids

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:03.080
that are important.

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:04.300
Now we're swimming around,

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:07.300
and we can see there's
another little structure

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:10.640
that is surrounded by a
membrane called the nucleus.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:11.473
So let's go through there.

00:32:11.473 --> 00:32:13.395
With our little paws, we can swim through.

00:32:13.395 --> 00:32:15.550
- This is what we remember,
from middle school biology,

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.570
is the brain of the cell.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:18.640
- It is the information,

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:20.930
the genetic information of the cell,

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:25.704
encoded in a six foot-long
strand of a chemical called DNA.

00:32:25.704 --> 00:32:26.537
- Wait a second.

00:32:26.537 --> 00:32:30.710
This is six feet of DNA in
this little microscopic...

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:31.720
You can't even see it.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:33.620
- And there's so much of it in the body,

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:36.300
it stretches from here to the
moon and back eight times.

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:38.970
And it all has to be packed
into very small space.

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:41.610
And so the cell doesn't
just shove this strand

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:43.510
willy-nilly into this structure.

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:45.890
It's not like me packing to go travel.

00:32:45.890 --> 00:32:47.860
It's actually neatly packed.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:49.140
And the way the cell does that,

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:52.520
is that it wraps the DNA around
proteins called histones.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.040
And those histones then
attract each other,

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.150
and eventually you get these
structures we call chromosomes,

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:59.610
which you can see
sometimes with your eyes,

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:01.290
but usually with a microscope.

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:03.440
But it doesn't all get bundled,

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:06.570
otherwise no genes could be read.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:09.850
Some of the genes have to
be opened up and unraveled,

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:11.993
unspooled, so that they can be turned on.

00:33:11.993 --> 00:33:13.950
- There's really good analogy for this,

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:18.730
if you existed during the 1970s and 1980s,

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:22.410
when cassette tapes were a thing.

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:24.360
- You might want to describe
what they were for our-

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:27.240
- Okay, so having
existed in these times...

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:30.380
For the children at home,

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:33.800
a cassette tape is a plastic cassette,

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:38.620
with a reel, on either
side, of magnetic tape.

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.930
But the important thing
here for this analogy,

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.713
is that if you turn it on its side,

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:46.680
that tape includes a lot of information,

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:49.203
but only a little bit is
readable at any given time.

00:33:49.203 --> 00:33:50.360
- And that would be a gene?

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:51.500
- And that would be a gene.

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:56.500
And so if your cell is
trying to read your code,

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.861
it's accessing just that
little bit at a time.

00:33:59.861 --> 00:34:01.490
It's not accessing all of it.

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:05.040
And that's actually what
helps cells know to be cells,

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.260
or the kind of cells they are.

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:07.200
- Well, it's essential.

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.460
When we're fertilized as
an egg, we can be anything,

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:12.490
but we need to have certain cell types.

00:34:12.490 --> 00:34:13.460
A brain cell has to know

00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:15.730
how to be a brain cell for
a hundred or more years,

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:17.180
and a liver cell has to stay a liver cell.

00:34:17.180 --> 00:34:18.013
If you wake up tomorrow

00:34:18.013 --> 00:34:19.870
and your brain is more like a skin cell,

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:20.910
you're in trouble.

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:23.660
So what happens is that
the packaging of the DNA

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:25.680
is specific to the cell type.

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:27.060
And every time that cell divides,

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:28.270
like in a skin cell,

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:29.640
it doesn't suddenly become a neuron,

00:34:29.640 --> 00:34:32.740
because the cells inherit the information

00:34:32.740 --> 00:34:35.340
that says that gene is for skin cells,

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:38.080
and keep that one on, but
don't turn on the brain cell.

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:41.100
- And they know this through epigenetics.

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.880
- Epigenetics is the other part
of the information in a cell

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:48.300
that controls which genes
are packaged tightly

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.690
or unfurled, unspooled,

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:51.820
like can happen if your cassette tape

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:52.770
gets stuck in the machine.

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:53.742
Do you remember when that used to happen?

00:34:53.742 --> 00:34:55.750
- I do, and then you'd have
to take the little pencil and-

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:57.570
- Wind it up, yeah, exactly.

00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:00.830
That's part of the problem
with aging, we think,

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:04.420
is that that beautiful
packaging of the DNA

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:07.860
that allows a cell to
remain a skin cell forever,

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:11.850
or brain cell essentially
forever, is lost.

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:14.650
You start to get this
unspooling of the DNA,

00:35:14.650 --> 00:35:16.310
and genes that shouldn't be on,

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:17.530
start to come on over time-

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:20.130
- Genes that shouldn't be
readable are now readable.

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:22.780
And so you have all this extra information

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:24.690
floating out there that
the cells can read.

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:26.650
And because the cells are now reading

00:35:26.650 --> 00:35:28.090
large parts of the code,

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:30.700
instead of the very
specific parts of the code,

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:33.100
they get confused?

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:34.070
- Right.

00:35:34.070 --> 00:35:35.910
And we call it X differentiation.

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:37.810
Differentiation is the common term

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:40.240
for cells becoming a certain cell type,

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:42.080
and we've coined the
term X differentiation,

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:44.750
which is essentially cellular confusion.

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:47.620
They become more of a
generalized cell type,

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:48.810
rather than a specific one,

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:50.320
because genes that shouldn't be on,

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:51.810
start to come on over time.

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:55.210
And that, we think, is a root
cause, if not the root cause,

00:35:55.210 --> 00:35:57.010
of aging itself.

00:35:57.010 --> 00:36:00.256
- Let's unpack that a little bit-

00:36:00.256 --> 00:36:01.423
- Excuse the pun.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.403
- Oh yeah, it is kind of a pun.

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:08.830
The thing that you've told me,

00:36:08.830 --> 00:36:13.830
and that really just
completely shifted the way

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:16.020
that I was thinking about aging,

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:19.030
even after actually working
with you for quite a bit,

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:22.900
is that this is not just
the root cause of aging.

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:26.980
You believe that X
differentiation is the root cause

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:31.323
of many, if not most diseases?

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:33.600
- I don't just believe it.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.500
I think it's now a fact.

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:37.440
When you look at Alzheimer's disease,

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:39.890
and unfortunately, up
until the last few years,

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:42.430
we were treating it at the end stage

00:36:42.430 --> 00:36:44.270
of the process of aging,

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:47.740
which results in plaques
and tangles and dementia,

00:36:47.740 --> 00:36:50.490
but the process that led to that disease,

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:53.240
was happening from birth and that's aging.

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:56.440
So Alzheimer's, just to take one example,

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:59.040
is an age-related disease
that's not just age-related,

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:02.560
actually 90% of it is
caused by the aging process.

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:05.710
This unspooling is X differentiation.

00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:07.990
If we were able to slow that down,

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:09.990
you'd have Alzheimer's much later.

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:11.280
And now we're showing in my lab,

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.490
and we'll talk more about this,

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:15.070
but I want to mention it
because it's so profound.

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:18.290
We can reverse the age
of a brain in an animal,

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:20.370
and when we do that, we're showing,

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:22.380
and we will continue to test this,

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:24.633
that those diseases go away,

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:29.010
proving that those diseases
are actually caused by aging.

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:33.130
- Okay, so let me see
if I can put this into

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:34.280
some kind of a picture.

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:37.090
We have these brain cells-

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:38.360
- Neurons, yeah.
- Neurons.

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:39.910
We have neurons.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:41.610
Over time,

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:45.150
because our DNA unspools,

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:47.303
it becomes less tightly packed.

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:53.800
The code is more readable.

00:37:53.800 --> 00:37:56.460
For instance, the code that
makes a liver, a liver,

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:57.710
or a kidney, a kidney.

00:37:57.710 --> 00:38:02.670
And so that brain cell is
sort of also part kidney cell?

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:05.480
- Right, and we can
read which genes are on.

00:38:05.480 --> 00:38:07.150
We call it gene expression patterns.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:07.983
It's very easy-

00:38:07.983 --> 00:38:11.030
- So you actually see this
in, in Alzheimer's brains.

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:13.130
You can see that the cell is-

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:14.120
- Losing its identity.

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:15.100
- Losing its identity?

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.840
- Right, losing its mind, so to speak.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:18.410
And we can see that with normal aging too,

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.100
even if you don't have Alzheimer's.

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:21.540
During aging, I'm 50 now.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:24.380
So some of my brain cells are
undoubtedly getting confused

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:25.980
and starting to believe

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:27.970
that they're part skin
cell, part kidney cell.

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:29.760
And that's probably the reason why,

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:30.620
even in your fifties,

00:38:30.620 --> 00:38:33.270
you cannot function like
you did in when you were 20.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:37.593
- Okay, that's dementia, that Alzheimer's.

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:40.860
What about other diseases?

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:43.200
Is there another one hat's
a good example for this?

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:44.500
Type 2 diabetes.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:45.730
- Right, high blood sugar?

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:47.740
- High blood sugar, same process.

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:49.190
- Yeah.
- Same problem.

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:50.023
- Well, it is.

00:38:50.023 --> 00:38:50.856
- These aren't two different diseases.

00:38:50.856 --> 00:38:51.689
They're the same thing?

00:38:51.689 --> 00:38:52.740
- That's a major point,

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:55.360
that we've been looking at
diseases as separate things.

00:38:55.360 --> 00:38:57.250
This is how modern medicine works.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:59.450
We need a different drug for
Type 2 diabetes right now,

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:03.850
and for Alzheimer's,
for cancer, for frailty,

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:05.750
muscle wasting, bone loss.

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:09.300
I'm saying that the same process

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:10.930
is leading to all of these diseases,

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:13.440
and if we can slow down, and even now,

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:16.170
we have a technology to
reverse that process,

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.670
those diseases will be slowed down,

00:39:18.670 --> 00:39:21.110
and even go away by age reversal.

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:23.930
- Okay, I'm on the cusp of this.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:27.710
I want you to spell it out for
me though, with the diabetes.

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:29.060
I like things in triplicate.

00:39:29.060 --> 00:39:31.340
So I'm convinced on the Alzheimer's.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:32.970
Explain what's happening in the diabetes,

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:34.760
that makes it the same process.

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:37.070
- Yeah, also, Type 2 diabetes, in part,

00:39:37.070 --> 00:39:40.020
is a problem of bringing glucose, sugar,

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:42.210
out of the bloodstream, into
your muscles and your brain,

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:47.210
in which a major requirement is glucose.

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:51.300
And if you don't suck the
sugar out of your bloodstream,

00:39:51.300 --> 00:39:53.610
you're going to have basically treacle,

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:55.040
or a caramelized protein,

00:39:55.040 --> 00:39:57.750
in your system, which is
going to cause all sorts of-

00:39:57.750 --> 00:39:59.640
- It sounds delicious, but
it's really a bad thing.

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:03.010
- No, you don't want to have
glucose attached to proteins.

00:40:03.010 --> 00:40:04.300
In fact, you can measure it.

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:07.460
Doctors, when they do a test called HbA1c,

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.520
that really means how much glucose

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:11.700
is physically bound to your hemoglobin,

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:13.540
which carries your oxygen.

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:14.770
And it's a percentage,

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:17.660
so if you have over
5.7% of your hemoglobin

00:40:17.660 --> 00:40:20.280
attached to glucose, you're pre-diabetic.

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:22.100
And over 6.5, I believe the number is,

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:25.240
is where you're actually
diabetic at that point.

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:26.930
And then you go on metformin.

00:40:26.930 --> 00:40:29.440
The point being that having
high levels of blood sugar,

00:40:29.440 --> 00:40:32.370
we know, predisposes
you to other diseases.

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:35.530
Heart disease, frailty, dementia.

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:38.480
You don't want to have a
lot of sugar in your blood.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:41.000
It's one of the reasons, I believe,

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:43.210
everybody should try to keep
their blood sugar levels

00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:44.810
reasonably low,

00:40:44.810 --> 00:40:47.220
because the higher levels are toxic,

00:40:47.220 --> 00:40:50.170
but what's happening in
terms of the aging process?

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:52.960
One of them is that the cells
that line the blood vessels

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:55.710
that suck the glucose out of your system,

00:40:55.710 --> 00:40:57.700
are losing their function.

00:40:57.700 --> 00:40:58.780
There's a particular gene

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:02.730
that's required to suck
glucose through the cell.

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:03.750
From outside in.

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:05.230
It's called GLUT4,

00:41:05.230 --> 00:41:07.700
It's a glucose transporter, number four.

00:41:07.700 --> 00:41:09.750
And over time with aging,

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:11.640
and particularly if you're
obese and you don't exercise

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:13.410
and don't all the good things,

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:17.150
your cells lose the ability to
make that GLUT4 transporter,

00:41:17.150 --> 00:41:19.070
and glucose will stay in
your bloodstream for longer,

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:19.903
which is toxic.

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:24.317
- You just said, just a couple
of moments ago, you said,

00:41:24.317 --> 00:41:27.103
"And then you take metformin,"
and it occurs to me

00:41:27.103 --> 00:41:30.170
that we got this metformin,
it activates AMPK.

00:41:32.060 --> 00:41:36.040
So is it un-X differentiating?

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:38.000
What is it doing?

00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:41.570
- Well, these defensive genes
seem to partially reset aging.

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:43.890
Sirtuins are part of the spooling process,

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:46.880
that with that cassette
tape, they bundle the DNA.

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:49.800
So by activating these
various longevity genes,

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:52.590
we're slowing down and
reversing some aspects

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:54.360
of that X differentiation process,

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:56.930
and getting the cells to
remember how to behave.

00:41:56.930 --> 00:41:58.430
- Okay.

00:41:58.430 --> 00:42:03.430
We did Alzheimer's, Type 2
diabetes, heart disease...

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:06.530
- Right.

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:10.140
- Heart disease is
cholesterol and bad food,

00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:13.320
and a lifetime of bad
exercise habits, right?

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:16.430
But you're about to tell
me it's not any of that.

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:17.780
It is that, but it's not that,

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:20.180
it's actually X differentiation?

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:21.240
- Yeah.

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:22.580
This is what we find

00:42:22.580 --> 00:42:25.120
is that we can actually now control aging,

00:42:25.120 --> 00:42:27.050
forwards and backwards, in mice at least.

00:42:27.050 --> 00:42:29.190
And we're doing experiments.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:31.420
Clinical trials are in humans now.

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:34.128
- Why did you do like clinical trials?

00:42:34.128 --> 00:42:35.960
[laughing]They're not real.

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:38.220
- Well, experiments in humans sounds bad,

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:39.650
but clinical trials is the real-

00:42:39.650 --> 00:42:40.820
- Literally, like people are going to take

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:42.207
that little section of
the tape and be like,

00:42:42.207 --> 00:42:45.300
"See, he doesn't really think
they're clinical trials."

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:47.150
- Well, no, these are all a double-blind,

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:49.550
placebo-controlled clinical
trials in humans, but in mice-

00:42:49.550 --> 00:42:50.383
- Oh, that's real.

00:42:50.383 --> 00:42:51.460
- Yeah.
- Okay.

00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:52.900
- So heart disease...

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:54.330
What we've shown in my lab is

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:55.970
because we can drive aging forwards,

00:42:55.970 --> 00:42:58.270
by disrupting the spools,

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:00.380
we can either reduce
the amount of sirtuins,

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:03.890
which causes them to spool
out and X differentiate,

00:43:03.890 --> 00:43:06.144
or we can create certain
types of stress on the cell

00:43:06.144 --> 00:43:08.630
that caused that problem as well.

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.870
Extreme stress, not the hormetic type.

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:13.300
We can accelerate aging in a mouse.

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:17.030
And one of the effects we get
is heart disease in a mouse.

00:43:17.030 --> 00:43:20.060
And so now, we have this evidence

00:43:20.060 --> 00:43:22.710
that disrupting the epigenome,

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:26.320
the readers of the DNA,
causing X differentiation,

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:28.220
is causing all of these diseases,

00:43:28.220 --> 00:43:30.500
including heart disease, in the mouse.

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:33.360
- Accelerating aging in a mouse

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:36.230
requires us to be able to measure aging.

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:39.150
One of the ways that we can do that,

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:42.040
and probably the one that
makes the most sense to people

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:43.320
in the way that we think about aging,

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:45.610
is you just look at a mouse, right?

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:47.910
It's got more gray hair,
it's becoming weaker.

00:43:47.910 --> 00:43:49.180
It gets confused, right?

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:50.590
It's not as perky and excited,

00:43:50.590 --> 00:43:53.160
and it doesn't run on a
little treadmill as long,

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:58.160
but there are now other ways
that we can measure aging.

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:02.873
And this is a really important point.

00:44:05.241 --> 00:44:07.090
This is this leads to the explosion.

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:07.923
- Yeah, right.

00:44:07.923 --> 00:44:12.090
So it was in 2013 a paper came
out from Steve Horvath's lab,

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:14.290
and there was another
paper by Greg Hannam,

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:17.870
that showed that if you
measure the chemicals

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:20.030
that are controlling this
bundling and spooling

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:23.070
of the genome to specify cell type,

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:27.670
there are certain sites on the
genes that you can measure.

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:30.640
The chemical is specifically
called DNA methylation.

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:31.473
It's very simple.

00:44:31.473 --> 00:44:33.610
It's just a carbon with three hydrogens.

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:37.070
This chemical cells add to the DNA.

00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:40.180
Stick to the DNA, as you're
developing in the womb,

00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:41.117
to make a brain cell
different than a liver cell,

00:44:41.117 --> 00:44:44.200
different pattern.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:45.280
But over time,

00:44:45.280 --> 00:44:47.670
there are changes that are predictable,

00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:50.410
and they change in a linear fashion,

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:53.660
so that if I took your
skin cell or your blood,

00:44:53.660 --> 00:44:56.350
and I measured the DNA methylation pattern

00:44:56.350 --> 00:44:57.183
across the genome,

00:44:57.183 --> 00:45:00.960
across that six feet in one
cell or in thousands of cells,

00:45:00.960 --> 00:45:02.460
which is how we do it,

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:04.330
I can then plug that into a program,

00:45:04.330 --> 00:45:07.620
and it'll spit out your
actual biological age.

00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:09.210
Not your birthday candles,

00:45:09.210 --> 00:45:10.870
which is based on how many times the earth

00:45:10.870 --> 00:45:12.060
has gone around the sun.

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:13.210
That's irrelevant.

00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:14.950
It's how old you really are

00:45:14.950 --> 00:45:18.640
which can predict also
when you're going to die.

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:20.250
- That's terrifying.

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:21.083
- No, it's not.

00:45:21.083 --> 00:45:23.470
It's liberating because we know now

00:45:23.470 --> 00:45:25.940
how to slow that process
down and even reverse it.

00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:29.960
We can get the DNA to spool less,

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:34.520
and also be repackaged to
reverse the aging process,

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:36.560
and get cells to work like they used to.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:38.170
So that number shouldn't be scary.

00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:40.350
It might be scary if there's
nothing you can do about it,

00:45:40.350 --> 00:45:42.550
but as we'll learn in this series.

00:45:42.550 --> 00:45:45.290
there are plenty of things
you can do about it.

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:46.920
- All of this as well and good

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:51.060
if we can all have our biological clocks

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:52.210
measured at any time,

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:55.550
but this sounds really
expensive and time consuming.

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:59.460
And it's a process and I'm
of course teeing you up here,

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:03.560
but this is also a
really important moment.

00:46:03.560 --> 00:46:06.180
- Well, it used to be
expensive just to read.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:09.080
The first human genome
was more than $2 billion,

00:46:09.080 --> 00:46:11.750
and took an army of people
and whole buildings.

00:46:11.750 --> 00:46:15.670
Now we can read a genome
on a Snickers-sized bar.

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:18.200
In a day, it's going to
be a hundred dollars,

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:19.710
probably next year.

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:21.120
And we use that same technology

00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:24.100
to read the DNA methylation patterns.

00:46:24.100 --> 00:46:26.650
We put it through what's
called a DNA sequencer.

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:27.483
And right now,

00:46:27.483 --> 00:46:29.950
it's a few hundred dollars
to run your sample,

00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:32.570
which is still beyond most people,

00:46:32.570 --> 00:46:35.170
if you want to do it every
week or even every month.

00:46:36.332 --> 00:46:38.250
There's a student in my
lab, Patrick Griffin,

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:43.250
who has just put up online
our pre-publication,

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:45.060
that shows that we can pull

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:47.330
thousands of people's samples together,

00:46:47.330 --> 00:46:50.140
and run them as one in
these sequencing machines.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.690
That brings the cost down

00:46:51.690 --> 00:46:54.210
to about less than a dollar to run this.

00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:55.043
Why is that important?

00:46:55.043 --> 00:46:58.510
It means that one day
anybody can do a cheek swab,

00:46:58.510 --> 00:47:00.090
and determine their biological age,

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:02.110
and figure out whether what
they're doing in their life

00:47:02.110 --> 00:47:03.910
is working or not to slow down aging.

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:05.130
- So theoretically,

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:08.313
if I wanted to check my
biological age every week,

00:47:09.580 --> 00:47:11.910
I could do that for 50
bucks a year, essentially.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:12.743
- Exactly.

00:47:12.743 --> 00:47:14.090
And eventually, it will
be a little home device.

00:47:14.090 --> 00:47:15.780
You can do it every
morning, if you feel like.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:16.950
- And your argument is,

00:47:16.950 --> 00:47:20.240
and I'm on board with this too,

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:24.590
this is far, far more
important to our understanding

00:47:24.590 --> 00:47:26.570
of our general sense of health,

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:30.593
and our general state of
propensity for disease,

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:35.100
than our chronological age otherwise?

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:36.360
- Right, and we find that people

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:37.680
who haven't done the right things,

00:47:37.680 --> 00:47:40.970
who haven't exercised and
haven't maintained a good diet,

00:47:40.970 --> 00:47:42.840
have an older biological age,

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:45.000
and will die sooner because of it.

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:48.113
We can now measure the
aging process with accuracy.

00:47:50.089 --> 00:47:51.040
It blows my mind

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:54.580
that we now have our
biological credit score.

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:56.700
Which, by the way, we can alter.

00:47:56.700 --> 00:47:58.020
Aging is malleable.

00:47:58.020 --> 00:48:00.160
That number doesn't stay the same,

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:02.817
and it doesn't have to
continue to tick up every year.

00:48:02.817 --> 00:48:04.573
- In yeast and mice.

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:06.613
- And in humans now.

00:48:08.330 --> 00:48:11.680
Greg Fahy, who's got a
company out on California,

00:48:11.680 --> 00:48:13.180
worked with UCLA researchers,

00:48:13.180 --> 00:48:16.410
in fact, the guy that co-invented
the clock, Steve Horvath.

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:18.040
They published last year

00:48:18.040 --> 00:48:21.530
that a triple treatment of chemicals.

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:25.710
I'll name them, DHEA,
metformin, and growth hormone.

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:26.900
It rebuilt the thymus,

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:29.970
which is something that
happens to all of us.

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:31.130
It gets smaller.

00:48:31.130 --> 00:48:31.963
But more importantly,

00:48:31.963 --> 00:48:34.650
they measured the blood biological clock,

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:37.110
to figure out what happened to their age.

00:48:37.110 --> 00:48:39.360
And they got younger by
two and a half years.

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:41.590
- After one treatment?

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:42.459
- It took a month-

00:48:42.459 --> 00:48:43.850
- So a series of treatments...

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:45.333
One series of treatments?
- Yeah.

00:48:45.333 --> 00:48:47.990
But here's what's fundamental about this.

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:49.620
You might say two and a
half years, who cares?

00:48:49.620 --> 00:48:51.480
First of all, I'll take an
extra two and half years.

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:53.770
I'll take an extra two
weeks if I can get it.

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:56.530
But two and a half years is
a big deal, for two reasons.

00:48:56.530 --> 00:49:00.690
One is Greg is now showing
that you can repeat this,

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:03.590
and go back not just two and
half years, but five years.

00:49:03.590 --> 00:49:06.440
I know of people that have
reversed their epigenetic clock

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:08.970
by 20 years over a year-

00:49:08.970 --> 00:49:10.770
- They had a measured pre-treatment?

00:49:10.770 --> 00:49:11.680
- [David] Right.

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:14.550
- They've gone through the same treatment?

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:17.060
The same treatment the
Greg used in the study,

00:49:17.060 --> 00:49:17.893
or similar treatment-
- Some people.

00:49:17.893 --> 00:49:21.527
Some people, but let's
talk about how we do that.

00:49:21.527 --> 00:49:23.340
There are a number of ways now

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:26.382
that seem to reverse the age of the body.

00:49:26.382 --> 00:49:27.640
It's an amazing-

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:29.610
- At least according to
these biological clocks

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:30.950
- Exactly.

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:32.870
We want to now figure
out, are they healthier?

00:49:32.870 --> 00:49:34.260
Do they look younger?

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:37.470
So far, anecdotally, the answer is yes.

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:38.303
But think about this.

00:49:38.303 --> 00:49:39.470
This is the second point that's important.

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:42.780
Two and a half years may not
be a big deal to some people,

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:46.840
some of our audience, but to be immortal,

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:49.340
all you have to do is go back
one year, every birthday.

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:50.173
- Oh well, that's good.

00:49:50.173 --> 00:49:51.006
So we're immortal now.

00:49:51.006 --> 00:49:51.839
Thank you, David.

00:49:51.839 --> 00:49:52.930
- Yeah, can we go home?

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:53.960
- [Matthew] Yeah, I
think we're done.[laughs]

00:49:53.960 --> 00:49:55.110
- We have some work to do.

00:49:55.110 --> 00:49:56.600
We definitely have work to do,

00:49:56.600 --> 00:49:58.110
especially when it comes to- .

00:49:58.110 --> 00:49:59.610
- This is going to freak people out,

00:49:59.610 --> 00:50:02.810
'cause they're going to say David
Sinclair, Harvard University,

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:04.380
just said that we can be immortal.

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.100
- Well, there's no reason why we cannot.

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:07.930
There's no law that says we have to age.

00:50:07.930 --> 00:50:10.630
And we now are learning,
not just what causes aging,

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:13.800
but how to reset the body
so that it's young again.

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:16.380
And we don't know how many
times you can reset the body,

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:18.430
but I'm betting that it's more than once.

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:19.460
So what happens in a world

00:50:19.460 --> 00:50:21.310
where you can reset
your age multiple times,

00:50:21.310 --> 00:50:22.730
maybe hundreds of times.

00:50:22.730 --> 00:50:24.520
That's when things get really interesting,

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:26.800
and I think when people
look at this podcast,

00:50:26.800 --> 00:50:28.650
and listen to it a hundred years from now,

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:29.483
they're going to say,

00:50:29.483 --> 00:50:33.640
"Yeah, that was the moment
when humanity really changed."

00:50:33.640 --> 00:50:38.640
- Okay, so let's encapsulate
this all together,

00:50:38.780 --> 00:50:41.010
Summarize.

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:45.263
This podcast episode is Why We Age.

00:50:46.270 --> 00:50:48.673
So if people want to come away from this,

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:51.240
instead of listening to the episode

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:52.450
again and again and again,

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:55.410
and trying to summarize
it, there's a lot here.

00:50:55.410 --> 00:50:56.860
Let's hit the points.

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:58.880
Why do we age?

00:50:58.880 --> 00:51:00.020
- Well, we now have an understanding

00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:01.480
that it's not just things go wrong.

00:51:01.480 --> 00:51:03.470
There's actual a fundamental process.

00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:04.960
This ticking of the clock,

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:07.010
the unspooling the genes
that happens in everything,

00:51:07.010 --> 00:51:11.140
from a tree to a yeast, to
a worm, mouse and ourselves,

00:51:11.140 --> 00:51:12.600
that's a fundamental cause of aging

00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:15.200
that leads to a lot of things going wrong.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:19.150
There are hallmarks of
aging that lead to disease.

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:22.040
And we'd see these in clinics.

00:51:22.040 --> 00:51:22.930
Doctors are trying to treat

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:25.750
the end stage of that
process, often too late,

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:28.450
putting a band-aid on the problem.

00:51:28.450 --> 00:51:29.500
What I'm saying,

00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:30.364
and what we've been discussing,

00:51:30.364 --> 00:51:32.880
is that there's a layer
above all these diseases

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:34.620
and the cause of aging,

00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:36.910
and that is the regulators, okay?

00:51:36.910 --> 00:51:38.430
So the regulators we've talked about.

00:51:38.430 --> 00:51:42.480
We've got mTOR, that
responds to amino acids,

00:51:42.480 --> 00:51:45.230
AMPK, which is blood sugar and energy,

00:51:45.230 --> 00:51:46.063
and sirtuins,

00:51:46.063 --> 00:51:48.280
which respond to both of
those things and more.

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:49.560
We'll talk about those other things

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:53.010
in another episode, like heat and cold.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:54.860
And what they do is that
they're slowing down

00:51:54.860 --> 00:51:55.940
all those bad processes.

00:51:55.940 --> 00:51:57.180
The main one,

00:51:57.180 --> 00:51:58.527
that's driving all these other bad things

00:51:58.527 --> 00:52:01.643
and these diseases we
call diseases of old age,

00:52:02.540 --> 00:52:05.570
is the X differentiation process.

00:52:05.570 --> 00:52:08.310
So what we really want to do
is to get this middle layer,

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:10.450
to be super potent,

00:52:10.450 --> 00:52:13.090
and slow down and even
reverse the aging process.

00:52:13.090 --> 00:52:14.320
So how do we do that?

00:52:14.320 --> 00:52:16.100
So now we have this upper
layer that's important.

00:52:16.100 --> 00:52:17.660
This is the environment.

00:52:17.660 --> 00:52:18.550
It turns out that

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:21.920
most of what regulates this
middle protective survival layer

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.020
is not the genetics that
you get from your parents.

00:52:26.020 --> 00:52:28.910
That's 20% of the control of aging.

00:52:28.910 --> 00:52:31.710
- We know that from a twin study

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:35.590
of really old data from Denmark.

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:38.920
- Yeah, there's a study by
Christensen et al.,2003,

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:41.560
that looked at Danish twins
that are genetically identical,

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:42.810
but they live different lifestyles.

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:45.570
They have different environments,
some smoked, some didn't,

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:47.140
some exercise, some didn't.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:51.370
And so now we know that if
you're genetically identical,

00:52:51.370 --> 00:52:54.520
20% of that, it determines
your health in later life,

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:55.780
slows down aging,

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:58.140
but 80% is in your own hands.

00:52:58.140 --> 00:52:59.230
How you live your life.

00:52:59.230 --> 00:53:01.750
And that's the top layer that
controls the longevity genes

00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:05.470
that controls the X differentiation
and disease process.

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:06.610
- So now we've got to figure out

00:53:06.610 --> 00:53:08.120
what to do in that top layer.

00:53:08.120 --> 00:53:10.370
Like, what are-
- We are in luck.

00:53:10.370 --> 00:53:11.780
- Most of this is in our hands.

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:13.357
- Well, a heck of a lot.

00:53:13.357 --> 00:53:14.507
80%.

00:53:14.507 --> 00:53:17.670
And we now are starting to
understand how to live a life,

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:19.760
to maximize your longevity,

00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:22.950
and slow down that X
differentiation aging process.

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:25.320
But we didn't cover sirtuins, in a way,

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:27.770
that I think we might've
lost a few people,

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:28.730
that the sirtuins,

00:53:28.730 --> 00:53:31.860
which is the third group
of these longevity genes,

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:33.200
how did they work?

00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:36.720
And so three of those
seven exist in the nucleus,

00:53:36.720 --> 00:53:39.330
to control that spooling process.

00:53:39.330 --> 00:53:42.000
So this X differentiation
process, actually,

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:45.200
is due to malfunctions in sirtuins,

00:53:45.200 --> 00:53:48.020
and we can maintain their
function during old age,

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:49.490
and even middle-age,

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:53.060
by exercising and dieting
and being hungry sometimes.

00:53:53.060 --> 00:53:54.240
Sirtuins will be activated,

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:56.420
and we'll talk about chemical
ways to do that later,

00:53:56.420 --> 00:54:00.330
but then they take care of
the packaging of the genome.

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:02.930
Remember I said, sirtuins or SIR,

00:54:02.930 --> 00:54:05.830
stands for silent information regulator?

00:54:05.830 --> 00:54:06.663
So what does that mean?

00:54:06.663 --> 00:54:10.690
Silent is the bundling of
the DNA silencing them,

00:54:10.690 --> 00:54:13.110
so you don't want the
liver gene on in the brain.

00:54:13.110 --> 00:54:16.130
Information is the DNA
and they regulate it.

00:54:16.130 --> 00:54:16.963
Sirtuins.

00:54:16.963 --> 00:54:17.870
It's in the name.

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:19.330
And in fact, in the 1990s,

00:54:19.330 --> 00:54:21.740
that's why I've been working ever since,

00:54:21.740 --> 00:54:24.090
on trying to understand, and test,

00:54:24.090 --> 00:54:25.950
my information theory of aging,

00:54:25.950 --> 00:54:28.080
which has only recently,
thanks to the book,

00:54:28.080 --> 00:54:30.620
become a very popular theory in biology.

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:32.390
And in fact, is now, I would say,

00:54:32.390 --> 00:54:34.950
the leading theory of why we age.

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:36.450
- And there's a really good metaphor.

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:37.283
We've used it before.

00:54:37.283 --> 00:54:38.120
You've used it a lot,

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:39.680
but I think it's worth repeating

00:54:39.680 --> 00:54:43.000
for this information theory.

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:46.030
And man, again, I apologize.

00:54:46.030 --> 00:54:47.870
Apologies to people who didn't grow up

00:54:47.870 --> 00:54:49.240
in the eighties and nineties,

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:50.073
but we're going to go now

00:54:50.073 --> 00:54:52.950
from cassette tapes to compact discs.

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:54.530
- CD's, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:56.700
- Well, these were amazing devices.

00:54:56.700 --> 00:54:58.900
You could fit about 10 songs on them.

00:54:58.900 --> 00:55:00.570
And the way that they work,

00:55:00.570 --> 00:55:03.380
is you've got digital
information in ones and zeros,

00:55:03.380 --> 00:55:04.920
similar to how DNA's encoded,

00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:08.090
which has four bases: chemical A-C-T-G.

00:55:08.090 --> 00:55:10.950
This digital information
is read by a laser,

00:55:10.950 --> 00:55:12.220
and it plays beautiful music.

00:55:12.220 --> 00:55:17.220
Let's say you've got a
symphony with 10 parts to it.

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:21.280
Each cell will play a different
combination of those songs,

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:23.270
but instead of there being 10,

00:55:23.270 --> 00:55:27.090
in a cell there's 20,000
different genes to play.

00:55:27.090 --> 00:55:28.420
What I'm saying with aging,

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:31.050
is that it's equivalent
having scratches on the CD,

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:33.620
so the reader, which is a laser,

00:55:33.620 --> 00:55:34.640
these days a blue laser,

00:55:34.640 --> 00:55:38.340
is skipping and playing the
wrong songs at the wrong time.

00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:39.230
And that sounds horrible.

00:55:39.230 --> 00:55:40.063
That's a cacophony.

00:55:40.063 --> 00:55:41.620
Nobody wants to listen to that.

00:55:41.620 --> 00:55:45.880
That, I'm saying, is the
equivalent of the aging process.

00:55:45.880 --> 00:55:48.130
And what we've been working
on for the last 20 years

00:55:48.130 --> 00:55:48.963
in my lab at Harvard,

00:55:48.963 --> 00:55:52.840
is how do you slow down those scratches?

00:55:52.840 --> 00:55:55.310
And also, can you polish the CD,

00:55:55.310 --> 00:55:59.000
so that those beautiful
symphonies are played again.

00:55:59.000 --> 00:56:00.360
- And we're going to come
back to this metaphor,

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:01.880
I think, probably again and again.

00:56:01.880 --> 00:56:04.880
It's sort of a point
that we can refer back to

00:56:04.880 --> 00:56:06.520
when we're talking about
all of these things

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:11.020
that we can do to get
rid of those scratches,

00:56:11.020 --> 00:56:15.980
to wipe away that epigenetic
junk, to reverse aging.

00:56:15.980 --> 00:56:20.730
- Right, and then diseases
of aging will go away.

00:56:20.730 --> 00:56:21.600
That's a big deal.

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:24.410
This is, I think, a turning
point in medical history,

00:56:24.410 --> 00:56:26.140
that we actually understand

00:56:26.140 --> 00:56:28.730
what causes those diseases fundamentally,

00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:32.870
not just trying to fix the
actual problem when it occurs.

00:56:32.870 --> 00:56:36.450
I liken this to get getting
to the edge of a cliff.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:38.040
And you wrote this in the book,

00:56:38.040 --> 00:56:40.970
is that we've been far too often

00:56:40.970 --> 00:56:44.220
working on trying to understand
why we falling off a cliff

00:56:44.220 --> 00:56:45.053
at the end of life,

00:56:45.053 --> 00:56:46.467
without even asking the question,

00:56:46.467 --> 00:56:47.980
"What brings us to the edge of that cliff

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:49.160
in the first place."

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:51.860
Now we finally understand
how we get to that cliff,

00:56:51.860 --> 00:56:53.360
and even how to prevent it.

00:56:53.360 --> 00:56:55.720
- And so at some point perhaps

00:56:55.720 --> 00:56:59.520
we don't have to worry about diseases

00:56:59.520 --> 00:57:02.210
because we're going to be so healthy.

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:06.190
So young that they're
not going to push us off.

00:57:06.190 --> 00:57:07.220
They're going to make us sick, maybe.

00:57:07.220 --> 00:57:08.070
We can treat that.

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:08.903
But-

00:57:08.903 --> 00:57:12.090
- Well, as we saw with
COVID-19, that if you're young,

00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:13.250
you can survive.

00:57:13.250 --> 00:57:14.490
So this is really important.

00:57:14.490 --> 00:57:16.870
It's not just about diseases of old age.

00:57:16.870 --> 00:57:20.600
Diseases throughout life can
be prevented and treated,

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:22.340
by harnessing this middle layer.

00:57:22.340 --> 00:57:24.800
These protective survival genes,

00:57:24.800 --> 00:57:28.430
mTOR, AMPK, sirtuins and others,

00:57:28.430 --> 00:57:29.820
not just with lifestyle changes,

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:30.653
which are pretty easy to implement.

00:57:30.653 --> 00:57:32.840
We can do that today.

00:57:32.840 --> 00:57:36.740
But also, increasingly, with nutrients,

00:57:36.740 --> 00:57:38.550
with drugs and supplements.

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:40.370
But it's messy world out there.

00:57:40.370 --> 00:57:41.290
And one are the things

00:57:41.290 --> 00:57:43.970
that we're going to do in this
podcast in later episodes,

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:45.760
is to talk about what's
true and what isn't.

00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:48.750
What supplements work, what
manufacturers you can trust.

00:57:48.750 --> 00:57:50.910
All of this is to clarify

00:57:51.756 --> 00:57:54.200
this really untrustworthy world out there,

00:57:54.200 --> 00:57:56.770
and talk about what is
known and what is not known,

00:57:56.770 --> 00:57:58.920
and what soon hopefully will be known.

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:02.090
- So we're going to talk a
lot about what people can do

00:58:02.090 --> 00:58:05.679
individually to mitigate aging.

00:58:05.679 --> 00:58:10.230
But there's one thing that you said

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:13.810
you think we should be doing
collectively, as a society,

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:15.590
to address this,

00:58:15.590 --> 00:58:19.080
to keep not just us individually
from that precipice,

00:58:19.080 --> 00:58:23.673
but to keep us as a people
from that precipice.

00:58:26.380 --> 00:58:28.680
- Well, there's one thing
that we've been ignoring

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:29.840
for centuries.

00:58:29.840 --> 00:58:32.210
Modern medicine doesn't even think of it.

00:58:32.210 --> 00:58:35.620
And that is that aging itself
is a medical condition.

00:58:35.620 --> 00:58:37.340
Aging is a disease.

00:58:37.340 --> 00:58:38.850
And as we've been talking about today,

00:58:38.850 --> 00:58:41.200
and we'll talk about in later episodes,

00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:44.560
it's increasingly a
treatable medical condition.

00:58:44.560 --> 00:58:45.600
So why is that important?

00:58:45.600 --> 00:58:48.090
Well, the main problem with the way we do

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:50.230
what I call whack-a-mole
medicine right now,

00:58:50.230 --> 00:58:52.390
is that you wait until you get sick.

00:58:52.390 --> 00:58:54.160
You go to the doctor, which
is often too late, right?

00:58:54.160 --> 00:58:56.000
Especially when it's cancer,

00:58:56.000 --> 00:58:59.030
but even heart disease is
hard to reverse, currently.

00:58:59.030 --> 00:58:59.863
If you go to the doctor,

00:58:59.863 --> 00:59:01.400
they'll treat that specific disease,

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:03.990
and ignoring what got you
there in the first place.

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:06.750
And you end up having healthy heart.

00:59:06.750 --> 00:59:07.880
We have pretty good drugs

00:59:07.880 --> 00:59:10.320
for blood pressure and heart disease.

00:59:10.320 --> 00:59:13.370
But that ignores the rest of the body.

00:59:13.370 --> 00:59:15.170
Our brains still gets old,

00:59:15.170 --> 00:59:18.770
and this is the worst
nightmare for society.

00:59:18.770 --> 00:59:20.680
No wonder we're seeing
an increase in dementia.

00:59:20.680 --> 00:59:23.410
It's because we're not
treating the whole body,

00:59:23.410 --> 00:59:26.820
but now that we understand
that all parts of the body,

00:59:26.820 --> 00:59:28.650
and essentially all diseases of old age,

00:59:28.650 --> 00:59:31.410
are due to this same underlying process,

00:59:31.410 --> 00:59:34.370
we can find ways to halt,

00:59:34.370 --> 00:59:37.130
certainly slow down, halt
and reverse this process,

00:59:37.130 --> 00:59:38.990
not just in one part of the body,

00:59:38.990 --> 00:59:41.120
but in all tissues and organs,

00:59:41.120 --> 00:59:42.360
so that we can stay young,

00:59:42.360 --> 00:59:45.400
not just with our cardiovascular system,

00:59:45.400 --> 00:59:46.760
but our brains as well.

00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:49.690
And when we do that,
that is true longevity.

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:53.577
That can get us a decades
longer, healthier life.

00:59:53.577 --> 00:59:55.320
- And we can get there together.

00:59:55.320 --> 00:59:57.110
- Well, that's what we're
going to talk about.

00:59:57.110 --> 00:59:58.650
It's not that difficult to be able

00:59:58.650 --> 00:59:59.483
to slow down the aging process.

00:59:59.483 --> 01:00:02.150
It's not that difficult to measure it.

01:00:02.150 --> 01:00:03.820
And we're going to learn how to do that.

01:00:03.820 --> 01:00:05.450
And this was just a primer.

01:00:05.450 --> 01:00:07.630
We're going to get into the
more details in later episodes,

01:00:07.630 --> 01:00:10.650
about practical things that
people can do every day

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:13.140
to be able to maximize their
health, their performance,

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:16.310
how they look and that
ultimately their longevity.

01:00:16.310 --> 01:00:17.770
- Once doctors understand
that we can do this.

01:00:17.770 --> 01:00:20.080
Once we all understand
that we can do this.

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:21.690
It becomes an imperative.

01:00:21.690 --> 01:00:25.280
- Well, I say it's not just
a right to live longer.

01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:26.870
It's actually your obligation,

01:00:26.870 --> 01:00:28.760
your duty to your family.

01:00:28.760 --> 01:00:31.870
I mean, it's not fair
to just abuse your body,

01:00:31.870 --> 01:00:35.820
or neglect it and have them
take care of you for 20 years

01:00:35.820 --> 01:00:37.160
in a nursing home.

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:40.400
We owe it to our families to
stay as healthy as possible,

01:00:40.400 --> 01:00:43.280
and as to society, as
productive as possible.

01:00:43.280 --> 01:00:44.840
And let's say we get another 30 years.

01:00:44.840 --> 01:00:47.610
Well, another 30 years buys you a chance

01:00:47.610 --> 01:00:49.880
to start a new career, to visit places,

01:00:49.880 --> 01:00:53.470
to educate your great
grandkids, to have a good time.

01:00:53.470 --> 01:00:55.490
We are going to have the type of lives

01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:58.060
that people can only dream of right now.

01:00:58.060 --> 01:01:01.920
- This is going to be fun,
these next seven episodes.

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:03.490
- I'm already having a lot of fun.

01:01:03.490 --> 01:01:08.380
We're also going to get into
some rather interesting research,

01:01:08.380 --> 01:01:12.340
about what works, and for
the most part, what doesn't.

01:01:12.340 --> 01:01:14.040
- And that's going to be, I think,

01:01:15.244 --> 01:01:16.460
challenging also to a lot of people

01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:19.900
who want a very quick and easy solution,

01:01:19.900 --> 01:01:24.900
Or want to believe what
they've they assume works.

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:28.320
- Well, there are a
lot of myths out there.

01:01:29.170 --> 01:01:33.660
Let's rattle some off
telomeres, antioxidants...

01:01:33.660 --> 01:01:34.940
These things, we're going to get into it,

01:01:34.940 --> 01:01:37.820
but there are so many things
that have become myths

01:01:37.820 --> 01:01:40.310
out there that do very
little for your longevity,

01:01:40.310 --> 01:01:43.680
but there are some things that
actually do work right now,

01:01:43.680 --> 01:01:45.700
and things that are just around the corner

01:01:45.700 --> 01:01:47.450
that will blow everybody's minds.

01:01:47.450 --> 01:01:48.283
- And the next episode,

01:01:48.283 --> 01:01:50.060
we're going to be talking specifically

01:01:50.060 --> 01:01:51.580
about dieting and fasting,

01:01:51.580 --> 01:01:53.430
and we don't want to give that away,

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:56.100
but that's that's up next, right?

01:01:56.100 --> 01:01:59.140
- It is, and so we'll see you in a week.

